<h3 id="taxonomy-weight-not-the-post-or-menu-item-weight">Taxonomy Weight (Not the post or menu item weight)</h3>
<h4 id="manually-specified-taxonomy-weights">Manually specified taxonomy weights</h4>
<h5 id="taxonomy-tags-weight-set-111">Taxonomy (tags) weight set 111</h5>
<h5 id="taxonomy-categories-weight-set-222">Taxonomy (categories) weight set 222</h5>
<h5 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight">Taxonomy weights and page weight</h5>
<h6 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-1">Taxonomy weights and page weight 1</h6>
<p>If you want to specify page weight along with taxonomy weights, <strong>and</strong>
if you do not use the <code>:page</code> keyword before the weight value, the
page weight <strong>must</strong> be specified as the very first weight.</p>
<h6 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-2">Taxonomy weights and page weight 2</h6>
<p>The page weight does not have to be specified as the very first weight
if using the <code>:page</code> keyword before the weight value.</p>
<h6 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-3">Taxonomy weights and page weight 3</h6>
<p>If you want to specify page weight along with taxonomy weights, <strong>and</strong>
if you do not use the <code>:page</code> keyword before the weight value, the
page weight <strong>must</strong> be specified as the very first weight, even when
you specify the weights separately on each line.</p>
<h6 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-4">Taxonomy weights and page weight 4</h6>
<p>The page weight does not have to be specified as the very first weight
if using the <code>:page</code> keyword before the weight value.</p>
<h4 id="auto-taxonomy-weight-calculation">Auto taxonomy weight calculation</h4>
<h5 id="tags-1">Tags</h5>
<h6 id="post-with-auto-taxonomy-tags-weight-calc-1">Post with auto taxonomy (tags) weight calc 1</h6>
<h6 id="post-with-auto-taxonomy-tags-weight-calc-2">Post with auto taxonomy (tags) weight calc 2</h6>
<h5 id="categories">Categories</h5>
<h6 id="post-with-auto-taxonomy-categories-weight-calc-1">Post with auto taxonomy (categories) weight calc 1</h6>
<h6 id="post-with-auto-taxonomy-categories-weight-calc-2">Post with auto taxonomy (categories) weight calc 2</h6>
<h5 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-5">Taxonomy weights and page weight</h5>
<h6 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-auto-1">Taxonomy weights and page weight (auto) 1</h6>
<p>If you want to specify page weight along with taxonomy weights, <strong>and</strong>
if you do not use the <code>:page</code> keyword before the weight value, the
page weight <strong>must</strong> be specified as the very first weight, even when
you specify the weights separately on each line.</p>
<h6 id="taxonomy-weights-and-page-weight-auto-2">Taxonomy weights and page weight (auto) 2</h6>
<p>The page weight does not have to be specified as the very first weight
if using the <code>:page</code> keyword before the weight value.</p>
<h2 id="dates">Dates</h2>
<h3 id="date">Date</h3>
<h4 id="just-date">Just date</h4>
<p>The <code>date</code> for this post is Hugo-compatible i.e. <a href="https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339#section-5.8">RFC3339-compliant</a>.</p>
<h4 id="date-set-using-org-time-stamp">Date set using Org time stamp</h4>
<p>The <code>date</code> for this post is set in Org using the <code>C-c . RET</code> binding.</p>
<h4 id="date--time">Date + Time</h4>
<h4 id="date--time-utc">Date + Time (UTC)</h4>
<h4 id="date--time-behind-utc">Date + Time (behind UTC)</h4>
<h4 id="date--time-after-utc">Date + Time (after UTC)</h4>
<h4 id="parsing-date-from-closed-property">Parsing date from CLOSED property</h4>
<p>When an Org TODO item is switched to the <code>DONE</code> state, a <code>CLOSED</code>
property is auto-inserted (default behavior).</p>
<p>If such a property is non-nil, the value (time-stamp) of that is used
to set the <code>date</code> field in the exported front-matter.</p>
<dl>
<dt>Reference</dt>
<dd><a href="https://orgmode.org/manual/Special-properties.html">(org) Special properties</a> or <code>C-h i g (org) Special properties</code></dd>
</dl>
<h4 id="parsing-date-for-a-post-which-begins-with-a-subheading">Parsing date for a post which begins with a subheading</h4>
<h5 id="the-closed-state-of-this-heading-which-is-nil-should-be-ignored">The “CLOSED” state of this heading (which is nil) should be ignored</h5>
<p>This is a test for .</p>
<h4 id="invalid-date">Invalid Date</h4>
<p>It’s possible that someone is using an existing Org file to export to
Hugo. Some exporters like <code>ox-texinfo</code> recognize dates of style
<code>YEAR1-YEAR2</code> to use them in Copyright headers.</p>
<p>But that date is invalid as per the standard date format used by Hugo
in <code>date</code> front-matter, and also as per <code>org-parse-time-string</code>.</p>
<p>So in that case, don’t allow <code>org-parse-time-string</code> to throw an error
and abort the export, but instead simply don’t set the <code>date</code> in the
front-matter.</p>
<p>In this post the <code>:EXPORT_DATE:</code> property is set to <code>2012-2017</code>, but
the export will still happen fine, with the <code>date</code> front-matter not
set.</p>
<h4 id="parsing-date-from-logbook">Parsing date from LOGBOOK</h4>
<div class="description">
<p>Parse the date from an entry like <code>"- State "DONE" from "DRAFT"
[2018-09-06 Thu 11:25]</code>.</p>
</div>
<p></p>
<p>In the case of multiple <code>"State "DONE" from .."</code> entries, use the
oldest entry to set the <code>date</code>.</p>
<h3 id="lastmod-date">Lastmod Date</h3>
<h4 id="lastmod-date-set-using-org-time-stamp">Lastmod date set using Org time stamp</h4>
<p>The <code>lastmod</code> for this post is set in Org using the <code>C-c . RET</code>
binding.</p>
<h4 id="lastmod-date-set-using-hugo-compatible-date-string">Lastmod date set using Hugo-compatible date string</h4>
<p>The <code>lastmod</code> for this post is Hugo-compatible i.e. <a href="https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339#section-5.8">RFC3339-compliant</a>.</p>
<h4 id="parsing-lastmod-from-logbook">Parsing lastmod from LOGBOOK</h4>
<div class="description">
<p>If LOGBOOK has multiple entries of <code>"- State "DONE" from .."</code>, use the
newest entry to parse the <code>lastmod</code> date from.</p>
</div>
<p></p>
<p>In the case of multiple <code>"State "DONE" from .."</code> entries,</p>
<ul>
<li>Use the newest entry to set the <code>lastmod</code>.</li>
<li>Use the oldest entry to set the <code>date</code>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <code>lastmod</code> field parsed from LOGBOOK transitions will have the
highest precedence. So it will <strong>not be auto-set</strong> even if the
<code>:EXPORT_HUGO_AUTO_SET_LASTMOD: t</code> property is set.</p>
<h3 id="publish-date">Publish Date</h3>
<h4 id="publish-date-set-using-org-time-stamp">Publish date set using Org time stamp</h4>
<p>The <code>publishdate</code> for this post is set in Org using the <code>C-c . RET</code>
binding.</p>
<h4 id="publish-date-set-using-hugo-compatible-date-string">Publish date set using Hugo-compatible date string</h4>
<p>The <code>publishdate</code> for this post is Hugo-compatible
i.e. <a href="https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339#section-5.8">RFC3339-compliant</a>.</p>
<h4 id="publish-date-set-using-scheduled">Publish date set using SCHEDULED</h4>
<p>If a subtree has the <code>SCHEDULED</code> <a href="https://orgmode.org/manual/Special-properties.html">Special Property</a> set, set the
<code>publishdate</code> front-matter to the scheduled date.</p>
<p>By default, <code>C-c C-s</code> in Org adds the <code>SCHEDULED</code> property to the
current subtree.</p>
<h3 id="expiry-date">Expiry Date</h3>
<h4 id="expiry-date-set-using-org-time-stamp">Expiry date set using Org time stamp</h4>
<p>The <code>expirydate</code> for this post is set in Org using the <code>C-c . RET</code>
binding.</p>
<h4 id="expiry-date-set-using-hugo-compatible-date-string">Expiry date set using Hugo-compatible date string</h4>
<p>The <code>expirydate</code> for this post is Hugo-compatible
i.e. <a href="https://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc3339#section-5.8">RFC3339-compliant</a>.</p>
<h3 id="customizing-date-format">Customizing date format</h3>
<h4 id="date-with-time-and-time-zone-default">Date with time and time-zone (default)</h4>
<h4 id="date-with-time">Date with time</h4>
<h4 id="date-with-only-date">Date with only date</h4>
<h2 id="preserve-filling-option">Preserve filling option</h2>
<h3 id="filling-is-preserved">Filling is preserved</h3>
<p>abc
def
ghi</p>
<p>–
Тест
Тест</p>
<h3 id="filling-is-not-preserved">Filling is not preserved</h3>
<p>abc
def
ghi</p>
<h3 id="chinese-locale">Chinese locale</h3>
<h4 id="filling-automatically-not-preserved-for-chinese-characters-preserve-filling-on">Filling automatically not preserved for Chinese characters (preserve filling on)</h4>
<div class="description">
<p>Ensure that multi-byte characters are force-unwrapped if the locale is
manually set or auto-detected as Chinese.</p>
</div>
<p>abc
def
ghi
这是一个测试
文本</p>
<p><a href="https://emacs-china.org/t/ox-hugo-auto-fill-mode-markdown/9547/5">Reference</a></p>
<h4 id="filling-automatically-not-preserved-for-chinese-characters-preserve-filling-off">Filling automatically not preserved for Chinese characters (preserve filling off)</h4>
<div class="description">
<p>Ensure that even when auto-unwrapping is enabled, no space is kept
between the unwrapped multi-byte characters if the locale is manually
set or auto-detected as Chinese.</p>
</div>
<p>abc
def
ghi
这是一个测试
文本</p>
